Task 1: Create Clones of Existing VMs
Subtask 1.1: Verify Lab Environment and Existing VMs
First, let's examine the current virtualization environment and identify available VMs for cloning.

Check virtualization support and services:
# Verify KVM support
lscpu | grep Virtualization

# Check if libvirt service is running
sudo systemctl status libvirtd

# Start libvirt if not running
sudo systemctl start libvirtd
sudo systemctl enable libvirtd
List existing virtual machines:
# List all VMs (running and stopped)
sudo virsh list --all

# Get detailed information about VMs
sudo virsh dominfo template-vm
Create a template VM if none exists:
# Create a simple template VM using virt-install
sudo virt-install \
  --name template-vm \
  --ram 2048 \
  --disk path=/var/lib/libvirt/images/template-vm.qcow2,size=20 \
  --vcpus 2 \
  --os-type linux \
  --os-variant centos8 \
  --network bridge=virbr0 \
  --graphics none \
  --console pty,target_type=serial \
  --location 'http://mirror.centos.org/centos/8-stream/BaseOS/x86_64/os/' \
  --extra-args 'console=ttyS0,115200n8 serial'
Subtask 1.2: Create Full Clone Using virsh
A full clone creates a complete, independent copy of the original VM.

Shutdown the source VM before cloning:
# Gracefully shutdown the template VM
sudo virsh shutdown template-vm

# Wait for shutdown to complete
sudo virsh list --all
Create a full clone using virt-clone:
# Create full clone with new name and storage
sudo virt-clone \
  --original template-vm \
  --name web-server-clone \
  --file /var/lib/libvirt/images/web-server-clone.qcow2

# Verify the clone was created
sudo virsh list --all
Create multiple clones for different purposes:
# Create database server clone
sudo virt-clone \
  --original template-vm \
  --name db-server-clone \
  --file /var/lib/libvirt/images/db-server-clone.qcow2

# Create application server clone
sudo virt-clone \
  --original template-vm \
  --name app-server-clone \
  --file /var/lib/libvirt/images/app-server-clone.qcow2
Subtask 1.3: Create Linked Clone Using qemu-img
Linked clones share the base image with the original VM, saving disk space.

Create a backing file snapshot:
# Create a snapshot of the original VM disk
sudo qemu-img create -f qcow2 \
  -b /var/lib/libvirt/images/template-vm.qcow2 \
  /var/lib/libvirt/images/linked-clone.qcow2

# Verify the backing file relationship
sudo qemu-img info /var/lib/libvirt/images/linked-clone.qcow2
Create VM definition for linked clone:
# Export original VM configuration
sudo virsh dumpxml template-vm > /tmp/template-vm.xml

# Create modified configuration for linked clone
sudo cp /tmp/template-vm.xml /tmp/linked-clone.xml

# Edit the configuration file
sudo sed -i 's/template-vm/linked-clone/g' /tmp/linked-clone.xml
sudo sed -i 's/template-vm.qcow2/linked-clone.qcow2/g' /tmp/linked-clone.xml

# Generate new UUID for the clone
NEW_UUID=$(uuidgen)
sudo sed -i "s/<uuid>.*<\/uuid>/<uuid>$NEW_UUID<\/uuid>/" /tmp/linked-clone.xml

# Define the new VM
sudo virsh define /tmp/linked-clone.xml
Subtask 1.4: Verify Clone Operations
Check disk usage and relationships:
# Compare disk sizes
ls -lh /var/lib/libvirt/images/

# Check backing file chains
sudo qemu-img info --backing-chain /var/lib/libvirt/images/linked-clone.qcow2
Test clone functionality:
# Start one of the cloned VMs
sudo virsh start web-server-clone

# Check VM status
sudo virsh list

# Connect to VM console (exit with Ctrl+])
sudo virsh console web-server-clone
